Let $\phi : X \dashrightarrow Y$ be an almost holomorphic dominant rational map between smooth complex projective varieties (i.e. $\phi$ is defined on a Zariski-open subset $U \subseteq X$ such that the restriction of $\phi$ to $U$ is proper over its image). Let $\pi : \Gamma \to X$ be a resolution of the indeterminacy of $\phi$, so that the induced map $\psi : \Gamma \to Y$ is a morphism and $\pi$ is a birational morphism from a smooth projective variety $\Gamma$. Assume that there exists an effective $\mathbb{Q}$-divisor $D$ on $\Gamma$ such that:
\begin{itemize}
\item the pair $(\Gamma, D)$ is klt (Kawamata log terminal), and
\item the $\mathbb{Q}$-divisor $-\pi_{*}(K_{\Gamma/Y} + D)$ on $X$ is nef, where $K_{\Gamma/Y} = K_{\Gamma} - \psi^{*}K_{Y}$ is the relative canonical divisor.
\end{itemize}

Question: Under these hypotheses on the relative anti-canonical divisor, does a structure theorem hold for $\phi$? Specifically, can one always find a smooth projective variety $Y'$ and a surjective holomorphic morphism $\phi' : X \to Y'$ such that $\phi'$ is birationally equivalent to $\phi : X \dashrightarrow Y$ (i.e. there exists a birational map $Y \dashrightarrow Y'$ making the appropriate diagram commute up to birational equivalence)?

